# 2. proving what a grammar generates
OK   L(G) = {0^m 1^2n : m >= n >= 0} on all strings of length <= 14
OK   the strings of L(G) of length <= 6 are as listed  [['', '0', '00', '000', '011', '0000', '0011', '00000', '00011', '000000', '000011', '001111']]
OK   0111 and 01111 are not generated (odd number of 1s; too few 0s)
OK   (a) the derivation built by the proof is valid and yields 0^m 1^2n (m >= n, m + 2n <= 16), in m + 1 steps
OK   (a) the cases m = 0 / m >= 1, n = 0 / m >= 1, n >= 1 are exhaustive, and m = 0 forces n = 0
OK   (a) in the third case (m-1, n-1) again satisfies m-1 >= n-1 >= 0, and 0 . 0^(m-1) 1^(2n-2) . 11 = 0^m 1^2n
OK   (a) derivation of 00011 (m = 3, n = 1): S, 0S11, 00S11, 000S11, 00011  [['S', '0S11', '00S11', '000S11', '00011']]
OK   (b) x in L implies 0x11 in L and 0x in L (|x| <= 12)
OK   (b) for x = 0^m 1^2n: 0x11 = 0^(m+1) 1^(2(n+1)) and 0x = 0^(m+1) 1^2n
OK   (b) every non-empty w in L(G) is 0x with x in L(G), or 0x11 with x in L(G) (|w| <= 12)
OK   (b trap) 0011 also has the derivation S, 0S, 00S11, 0011 (its first rule is S -> 0S)
OK   (b) 011 = 0 . eps . 11: the inner string may be empty
OK   (trap) adding S -> SS breaks the claim: 0110 is generated but is not in L
OK   (c) 0011 has exactly two parse trees
OK   (c) 0^m 1^2n has exactly C(m, n) parse trees (m + 2n <= 14)
OK   (c) S -> 0S | T, T -> 0T11 | eps generates the same language (|w| <= 14)
OK   (c) ... and every string of the language has exactly one parse tree in it (|w| <= 14)

